    *   **NVIDIA Jetson Orin Nano:** The gold standard for "learning AI" on robots. It has a GPU specifically designed to run AI models locally.
*   **The Workflow:** The AI model runs on the Jetson/Pi, which then sends a command (e.g., "Turn Left") to the Arduino, which physically moves the motors.

### 2. Add "Senses" (Input Data)
A learning model needs data to learn from. A standard BB-8 model is "blind." To embed AI, you must add sensors that provide a stream of data.

*   **For Vision (Object Detection/Following):** 
    *   Add a **Camera** (Raspberry Pi Cam or a USB Webcam) inside the head. 
    *   *AI Application:* Use **YOLO (You Only Look Once)** or **TensorFlow Lite** to let BB-8 recognize your face or follow a specific colored ball.
*   **For Spatial Awareness (SLAM/Mapping):**
    *   Add a **LiDAR sensor** (like the RPLidar A1) or an **Ultrasonic sensor array**.
    *   *AI Application:* Implement **SLAM (Simultaneous Localization and Mapping)** so the robot can learn the layout of your room and navigate autonomously.
*   **For Balance and Orientation:**
    *   Ensure there is an **IMU (Inertial Measurement Unit)** like the MPU-6050.
    *   *AI Application:* Use **Reinforcement Learning (RL)** to help the robot learn how to recover its balance if it gets pushed.

### 3. Precision Control (The Feedback Loop)
Most basic BB-8 builds use simple DC motors. AI models struggle with these because the AI doesn't know *exactly* how far the robot has moved (it just knows the motor is "on").

*   **The Change:** Upgrade to **Motors with Encoders**.
*   **Why:** Encoders tell the AI exactly how many rotations the wheel has made. This "feedback loop" is critical for the AI to learn a map of the room or to move in a perfectly straight line.

### 4. Software Architecture (The "Glue")
You cannot simply "upload" an AI model to a robot like you do with a sketch in Arduino. You need a middleware.

*   **The Change:** Install **ROS 2 (Robot Operating System)**.
*   **What it does:** ROS 2 is the industry standard. It allows your AI model (running in Python/C++) to communicate with your hardware (the motors and sensors) in real-time.

